WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
Most interfaces need far fewer
font sizes than they use.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.400
An audit of a mature product often
finds fifteen or more sizes,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.480
many of them a pixel apart.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:15.060
A type scale replaces that drift
with a short, deliberate list.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.160
You will pick a base and a
ratio, name the steps by role,

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.300
and apply three of them to a modal.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.200
A modular scale multiplies
a base size by a ratio.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:31.700
With a 16 pixel base and a ratio
of 1.25 you get roughly 12.8,

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:37.820
16, 20, 25, 31, and 39.
Round those to whole

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.840
pixels and you have a scale.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.140
The ratio guides the feel.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:48.900
From 1.125 to 1.2 is compact, good
for dense dashboards and tools.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.060
1.25 is a balanced default
for most products.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.980
1.333 and up is dramatic, better
suited to marketing pages with

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.360
big headlines. You can use a tighter
ratio inside the app and a larger one for

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.420
marketing, as long as
they share the same base.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.600
Name steps by role, not by size.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.430
Names like text-20 invite
people to add text-21.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.240
Role names keep the list closed.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:23.320
Five steps: caption, body,
lead, title, display.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.840
Use rem so the scale follows
the reader’s browser font size.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:32.060
People who set a larger default are
often the ones who need it most.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.240
If someone sets their root to 20
pixels, every step grows with them.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.560
Before, a modal used 15 pixels
for body, 17 for the title,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.840
and 13 for helper text, chosen one
at a time over several sprints.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.660
Nothing reads as a decision.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.400
After, the modal uses three
steps from the scale.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.320
Title, body, caption. The
title is clearly a title.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.440
The helper is clearly secondary, instead
of almost the same size as the body.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.760
For large display text you can let
the size grow with the viewport.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.940
Clamp between 2 rem and 3 rem.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:17.080
Keep body text fixed. Fluid body sizes make
line length and spacing harder to reason

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.380
about, and the gain is small.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:25.120
Pick a base and a ratio, round the
results, and commit to five or six steps.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.700
Name steps by role so
the list stays short.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.840
Use rem, and reserve fluid
sizing for display text.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:38.250
Next: a good size can still be tiring to
read if the line is too long or the line

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.140
height does not match.
